[B]Methodology and honest caveats.[/B]
[LIST]
[*]Architecture-matrix verdicts marked 📖 are read from the plugin's own published documentation; ❓ verdicts are inferred from the plugin's known architecture and have not been independently verified. 🧪 verdicts are measured against the in-tree RTP harness on the same hardware.
[*]Numeric metrics are intentionally unfilled in this beta. A dedicated stress-test helper plugin is in development to drive identical workloads through every plugin in the table; results will be backfilled (with raw logs published) before `3.0.0` final.
[*]Competitor plugins update frequently. This comparison reflects published platform support and observed behavior at the time of writing — corrections welcome via GitHub issue, with a link to the contradicting documentation or a reproduction.
[*]Feature breadth, GUI, and claim-plugin integration are [I]not[/I] benchmarked here. Several competitors trade speed for those features, and that's a legitimate choice — the table is about throughput and platform fit, not feature parity.
[/LIST]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#16A085]Features[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][B]Any number of teleport regions per world.[/B] Each region has its own shape (square / circle / rectangle), radius, center, curve weighting, vertical bounds, world override, and permission gates.
[*][B]Vertical adjustors[/B] — Linear and Jump. Useful for sky islands, void worlds, and Nether ceilings.
[*][B]Multi-dimensional support[/B] — Overworld, Nether, End, custom dimensions all work.
[*][B]Hot-reloadable configuration[/B] via `/rtp reload` (and the experimental `/rtp config` editor).
[*][B]Teleport safety[/B] — configurable radius check, optional emergency landing platform, configurable invulnerability timer, flat material allow/deny list. ([I]Vanilla block tags and state predicates are an [B]RTP-Pro[/B] feature.[/I])
[*][B]Pre-loading & queues[/B] — async chunk loading, per-player queues, global queues, reuse of locations from cancelled teleports, fairness-preserving allocation.
[*][B]Self-improving[/B] — bad-placement memory persists across restarts (YAML-backed; no database to set up).
[*][B]Configurable messages[/B] in `messages.yml` (English).
[*][B]Permission-based sounds and effects[/B] — see the [URL='https://github.com/dailystruggle/RTP/blob/main/docs/admin/FOR_SERVER_ADMINS.md']admin guide[/URL] for the syntax.
[*][B]Public API[/B] for addon developers — events, shapes, lifecycle hooks. Examples in the GitHub repository.
[*][I]Not in Lite — available in [B]RTP-Pro[/B]:[/I] SQL/Redis backends, Folia adapter, claim-plugin integrations (GriefDefender, GriefPrevention, Lands, WorldGuard, Towny, Factions, HuskTowns, RedProtect), Vault economy, multilingual `lang/**`, login-reserve cache, visitor mode, vanilla block tags / state predicates in `safety.yml`.
[/LIST]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#16A085]Safety List[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
`safety.yml` in Lite accepts a flat material allow/deny list — plain material names, one per line:

[LIST]
[*][B]Examples:[/B] `LAVA`, `MAGMA_BLOCK`, `CACTUS`, `FIRE`
[/LIST]

Unknown materials log a single warning — never silently dropped.

[I]Need vanilla block tags (e.g. `#minecraft:leaves`), state predicates (`OAK_SLAB[waterlogged=true]`), or wildcards (`*[waterlogged=true]`)? That richer grammar ships in [B]RTP-Pro[/B].[/I]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#9B59B6]FAQ[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]

[B]Q: This responds way faster than other RTP plugins. Why aren't I waiting for chunk loading?[/B]
A: When I say [I]optimized[/I], I mean optimized. In most cases there's already a verified destination ready in the queue before you even type the command. The chunks are pre-loaded; the safety check is already done.

[B]Q: How do I set up teleportation between worlds?[/B]
A: See the [URL='https://github.com/dailystruggle/RTP/blob/main/docs/admin/FOR_SERVER_ADMINS.md']admin guide[/URL]. World resolution order on command: player's current world (or `world:` parameter) → world's target region → region's target world.

[B]Q: Does it work with Iris / Terra / custom datapack generators?[/B]
A: Yes. Region files are read directly, so modded and namespaced biome and block IDs are preserved. No special configuration needed.

[B]Q: What's the difference between RTP and RTP-Pro?[/B]
A: Same engine, same source tree. [B]RTP-Pro[/B] ships first on each release and includes SQL/shared-database backends for multi-server / proxy setups, plus priority support. The free [B]RTP[/B] (Lite) jar ships hours-to-days later with the same core engine and is fully sufficient for single-server deployments.

[B]Q: Can I upgrade from Lite to Pro later?[/B]
A: Yes — drop in the Pro jar over the Lite one. Same configuration, same data files, same commands.

[B]Q: I'm on Forge / NeoForge.[/B]
A: Run [B]Arclight[/B] or [B]Mohist[/B] (Bukkit-compatibility launchers, both officially supported) and use the Spigot/Paper build of RTP. A native Forge adapter is not planned and is lower priority than multi-server / proxy support.

[B]Q: Memory and MSPT — should I worry?[/B]
A: RTP trades memory for speed: the queue lives in RAM and the plugin keeps a small spatial-memory cache. On a healthy server you should not see TPS drop below ~19 from RTP alone, but [I]high MSPT spikes around new-area generation are expected[/I] — that's the cost of generating the chunks. The queue smooths it out so each individual `/rtp` doesn't pay the full cost.

[B]Q: How do I report a bug?[/B]
A: Open a GitHub issue with your server version, RTP version, platform (Spigot/Paper/Folia), the relevant config files, and the error log section. Discord is also fine for general questions; bug reports belong on GitHub so they don't get lost.

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#2980B9]Reference[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]

[SPOILER='Basic commands']
[LIST]
[*][B]`/rtp`[/B] — teleport to the default region for your current world.
[*][B]`/rtp [parameter]:[value]`[/B] — specify region, world, player, and/or temporary region parameters before teleporting.
[*][B]`/rtp reload`[/B] — reload all configuration from disk.
[*][B]`/rtp scan start|pause|resume|reset|cancel`[/B] — pre-warm the spatial-memory cache by walking a region. (Renamed from `/rtp fill` in 2.x.)
[*][B]`/rtp config`[/B] — interactive config editor. [I]Experimental in `3.0.0-beta.1`[/I]; surface and grammar may change before `3.0` final. For production, edit YAML directly.
[/LIST]
Console commands work but may require an explicit `player:` / `world:` / `region:` parameter, since the console is neither a player nor in a world.
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='PlaceholderAPI placeholders']
[LIST]
[*][B]`%rtp_player_status%`[/B] — the player's current teleport status (idle, waiting, teleporting, etc.).
[*][B]`%rtp_total_queue_length%`[/B] — total locations available to the player in the region `/rtp` would use.
[*][B]`%rtp_public_queue_length%`[/B] — locations available to everyone in that region.
[*][B]`%rtp_personal_queue_length%`[/B] — locations reserved specifically for that player in that region.
[*][B]`%rtp_teleport_world%`[/B], [B]`%rtp_teleport_x%`[/B], [B]`%rtp_teleport_y%`[/B], [B]`%rtp_teleport_z%`[/B] — last/current teleport destination.
[/LIST]
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='Soft dependencies (optional)']
[LIST]
[*][B]PlaceholderAPI[/B] — exposes the placeholders above.
[*][B]ProtocolLib[/B] — used by some effects.
[/LIST]
[I]PaperLib is no longer required — Paper provides native async chunk APIs.[/I]
[I]Vault economy is not bundled in Lite. To charge for `/rtp`, use [B]RTP-Pro[/B].[/I]
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='Known limitations in 3.0.0-beta.1']
[LIST]
[*]Lite is not yet on a fixed publishing cadence; first regular Lite release lands at `3.0` GA.
[*]Fabric is not functional in the beta — planned for a later release. Don't deploy on Fabric.
[*]No Folia in Lite. The Folia adapter is an [B]RTP-Pro[/B] feature; on Folia, `/rtp` will not function.
[*]No SQL/Redis backends in the Lite assembly (H2, SQLite, MySQL, PostgreSQL, Jedis are stripped). The default YAML-file database persists learned state across restarts and is fine for single-server deployments. Shared-database / multi-server backends are an [B]RTP-Pro[/B] feature.
[*]No claim-plugin integrations, Vault economy, multilingual support, or login-reserve cache in Lite — all [B]RTP-Pro[/B] features.
[*]The `/rtp config` command is experimental — surface, argument grammar, and write-back semantics may change before `3.0` final. Edit YAML directly for production.
[*]Edits to `safety.yml` and biome filters do not yet invalidate the persisted shape cache. Workaround: `/rtp scan reset <region>` after changing safety predicates.
[*]Emergency landing platform default changed: `safety.yml` now ships `platformRadius: -1` (disabled). Set it to `0` or higher to restore legacy 2.x behavior.
[/LIST]
For the live list, see the [URL='https://github.com/dailystruggle/RTP/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md#known-issues']CHANGELOG[/URL].
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='A note on AI assistance']
I use AI coding assistants (LLM-based pair-programming tools) for scaffolding, refactoring, test generation, and documentation drafting. Every line that ships is reviewed, tested, and signed off by me. Architecture, safety invariants (thread model, chunk-ticket lifecycle, claim checks), and release decisions are mine alone. No AI output is published without human verification against the requirements and test suite. If you prefer plugins written without AI tooling in the loop at all, this isn't that plugin, and I'd rather you know up front.
[/SPOILER]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#27AE60]Outgrown Lite?[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
[B]RTP-Pro[/B] is a drop-in upgrade — same configuration, same data files, same commands. Pro adds SQL/shared-database backends for multi-server / proxy setups, earliest releases on each version, and priority support within a documented response window.

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

